gs_simulation
=============

This project provides original Python implementations of methods, models, and
algorithms from the field of grey systems analysis. The code itself is licensed
under the MIT License (see LICENSE).

PRIMARY REFERENCE
-----------------

The methods implemented here follow the canonical exposition in:

    Sifeng Liu (2024). Grey Systems Analysis: Methods, Models and Applications.
    2nd Edition. Springer, Singapore.
    ISBN 978-981-97-8726-5 (print) / 978-981-97-8727-2 (eBook)
    DOI: https://doi.org/10.1007/978-981-97-8727-2
    Open Access under Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial-NoDerivatives
    4.0 International License (CC BY-NC-ND 4.0).

Mathematical formulations of grey systems methods are not copyrightable
(per Baker v. Selden, 101 U.S. 99, 1879, and 17 U.S.C. § 102(b)). The original
exposition, prose, figures, and worked-example datasets in Liu (2024) remain
under the book's CC BY-NC-ND 4.0 license. Code in this repository is original
and does not reproduce book content.

We attribute Liu (2024) as the source of method derivations used in the
docstrings and tests. Each module's docstring identifies the relevant chapter
and section. Worked-example test cases that use specific input values from the
book are clearly labeled and cited; the inputs themselves are factual data
arrangements not protected by copyright.

ATTRIBUTION TO ANTECEDENTS
--------------------------

Grey system theory was founded by:

    Julong Deng (1982). "Control problems of grey systems."
    Systems & Control Letters, 1(5), 288-294.

Subsequent foundational work includes contributions from Sifeng Liu, Yi Lin,
Jeffrey Forrest, Yingjie Yang, Naiming Xie, Bo Zeng, and the broader grey
systems research community across more than 130 countries.

